are humans the only species that can  learn to decipher those special stimuli  that are so familiar to us printed words  to examine this we taught a group of  baboons to discriminate real English  words from nonsense strings of letters  which we will call non words and doing  this was only possible because of the  extraordinary research facility  developed by co-author Joelle beigel or  behavioral experiments with baboons  there are 10 testing booths indicated on  one side of a large enclosure and so  boone's can freely enter a testing booth  take part in an experiment and quit when  they feel like it each booth is equipped  with a touchscreen that's used to  present the test stimuli and to record  responses and a food dispenser to  deliver award after each correct  response but baboons are identified by a  microchip implanted in each arm a  computer then launches a particular  testing program initiating a new  experiment or picking up with a baboon  left off the last time this unique  facility allows us to collect data for  several thousands of trials per baboon  each day in optimal conditions with no  stress to the animal certainly reading  is one of the most complex skills that  humans have to learn and in languages  like English and French we read these  words via their component letters that  is we use information about which  letters of we're in the word in order to  recognize it but perhaps this is only  part of the story there might be some  other predisposition toward letter based  reading that has nothing to do with  language in our experiment baboons first  saw a string of four letters on the  screen touched that stimulus in order to  initiate the trial and then had to touch  an oval shape on the screen of the  letters formed a real English word or a  cross shape if the letters were not a  real  the key to success in training babu's to  discriminate between real words and non  words is that each new word the baboon  had to learn was repeatedly presented  intermixed with different non words so  the baboon learned to respond  differently to the repeated stimulus our  star baboon Dan learned to respond  correctly to more than 300 words in  about six weeks of training and  approximately 50,000 trials here we can  see how accuracy of responding to two  words and two non words evolves as a  function of training for the six baboons  tested in our study we measured the  orthographic distance between each  non-word and the set of words that a  baboon had learned who use the standard  edit distance that measures the number  of changes that is letter deletions  letter insertion substitutions and  transpositions reported transform them  onward into real word now on this graph  here we can see how the greater the  orthographic distance between the non  word and was known to the bones the  easier it was to respond that this  stimulus was an awkward accuracy  increasing as a function of automatic  distance and on the right here we see  the same linear relation between  accuracy in responding to long words and  orthographic distance between the non  words and words known to human subjects  they take this as evidence that our  baboons were using orthographic  information that is information about  letter identities and letter positions  in order to accurately discriminate real  words from non words in other words we  have evidence for a predisposition  toward letter based reading that is not  linguistically based and not even  specific to humans
 

